          ":question/id": "18a41cc2-6786-5c7c-b54f-7a6c8dbb881f",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-15476",
          ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({e}^{t})},\\,y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061({e}^{t}),\\,-\\infty <t<1,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({e}^{t})}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061({e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({e}^{t})}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({e}^{t})}}, \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}(t) & =-{e}^{t}{csc}^{2}\u2061({e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-{e}^{t}{csc}^{2}\u2061({e}^{t})}{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({e}^{t})}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({e}^{t})}}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({e}^{t})}}{{sin}^{2}\u2061({e}^{t})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({e}^{t})} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2}{{sin}^{3/2}\u2061({e}^{t})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({e}^{t})}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{2}{{sin}^{3/2}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3/2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {2}^{3/2}\\,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-8\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
