          ":question/id": "df476431-ab3a-5147-98f8-ef5f1e16d04c",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-353353",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/85/853358cc3207d3f2738ecc3bd2ee6391d62b1a51735c43e16f37a2b83414800e.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3t+1),\\,y=t{e}^{t},\\,-\\frac{1}{3}<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=\\frac{3}{3t+1},\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=(t+1){e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(t+1){e}^{t}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{3t+1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t+1)(3t+1){e}^{t}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=0,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,0} & =\\frac{(0+1)(0+1){e}^{0}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        },
