          ":question/id": "ef5ed0c9-8fa3-5025-804c-fe51ae9e2b6d",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-15477",
          ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={sec}^{2}\u2061t,\\,y={csc}^{3}\u2061t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=2{sec}^{2}\u2061t\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=-3{csc}^{3}\u2061t\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-3{csc}^{3}\u2061t\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t}{2{sec}^{2}\u2061t\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t} \\\\  & =-3{csc}^{3}\u2061t\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}\u2061t\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}\u2061t{cot}^{2}\u2061t{cos}^{2}\u2061t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{3}\u2061t}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}\u2061t}{{sin}^{2}\u2061t}\\right){cos}^{2}\u2061t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{4}\u2061t}{{sin}^{5}\u2061t}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}\u2061t{cot}^{4}\u2061t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){cot}^{4}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\, \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        }
      ],
